text=Comentário do Lucas...usou Edubuntu com LTSP5. a instalação foi ridícula de fácil...auto configurou a eth0 e eth1, mas o ltsp5 esta com um boot mais lento nos terminas em relação ao LTSP4.2.%0a%0aInstalação feita em Ubuntu 7.04 %0a%0a/usr/share/doc/ltsp-server/QuickInstall%0a[@sudo apt-get install ltsp-server-standalone openssh-server%0a%0aNow create your Thin Client environment on the server by running:%0a%0asudo ltsp-build-client@]%0a%0a%0aUbuntu Server já vem com LTSP5%0a%0aUbuntu Server edition includes thin client support using LTSP (Linux Terminal Server Project). LTSP-5, the latest release, offers a simple installation and easy maintenance.\\%0ahttp://www.ubuntu.com/products/WhatIsUbuntu/serveredition%0a%0a'''UbuntuLTSP\\%0ahttps://help.ubuntu.com/community/UbuntuLTSP\\%0ahttp://doc.ubuntu.com/edubuntu/handbook/C/'''%0a%0aDocs.\\%0ahttps://help.ubuntu.com/community/LTSPServerSetup\\%0ahttps://help.ubuntu.com/community/UbuntuLTSP/Tour\\%0ahttp://doc.ubuntu-fr.org/serveur/ltsp\\%0ahttp://www.edubuntu.org/GettingStarted\\%0ahttp://www.edubuntu.org/ThinClientConfig%0a%0a%0a'''Arquivos de Conf'''%0a%0anano /etc/default/tftpd-hpa%0a[@#Defaults for tftpd-hpa%0aRUN_DAEMON="no"%0aOPTIONS="-l -s /var/lib/tftpboot"@]%0a%0a/var/lib/tftpboot/ltsp/i386# ls%0a[@abi-2.6.20-15-386     initrd.img                nbi.img                pxelinux.0    System.map-2.6.20-15-386  vmlinuz-2.6.20-15-386%0aconfig-2.6.20-15-386  initrd.img-2.6.20-15-386  nbi.img-2.6.20-15-386  pxelinux.cfg  vmlinuz@]%0a%0a%0acat /etc/network/interfaces%0a[@auto lo%0aiface lo inet loopback%0a%0aauto eth1%0aiface eth1 inet dhcp%0a%0aauto eth0%0aiface eth0 inet static%0a        address   192.168.0.1%0a        netmask   255.255.255.0%0a        network   192.168.0.0%0a        broadcast 192.168.0.255@]%0a%0a%0acat /etc/dhcp3/dhcpd.conf%0a[@#%0a# Sample configuration file for ISC dhcpd for Debian%0a#%0a# $Id: dhcpd.conf,v 1.1.1.1 2002/05/21 00:07:44 peloy Exp $%0a#%0a%0a# The ddns-updates-style parameter controls whether or not the server will%0a# attempt to do a DNS update when a lease is confirmed. We default to the%0a# behavior of the version 2 packages ('none', since DHCP v2 didn't%0a# have support for DDNS.)%0addns-update-style none;%0a%0a# option definitions common to all supported networks...%0aoption domain-name "example.org";%0aoption domain-name-servers ns1.example.org, ns2.example.org;%0a%0adefault-lease-time 600;%0amax-lease-time 7200;%0a%0a# If this DHCP server is the official DHCP server for the local%0a# network, the authoritative directive should be uncommented.%0a#authoritative;%0a%0a# Use this to send dhcp log messages to a different log file (you also%0a# have to hack syslog.conf to complete the redirection).%0alog-facility local7;%0a%0a# No service will be given on this subnet, but declaring it helps the%0a# DHCP server to understand the network topology.%0a%0a#subnet 10.152.187.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a#}%0a%0a# This is a very basic subnet declaration.%0a%0a#subnet 10.254.239.0 netmask 255.255.255.224 {%0a#  range 10.254.239.10 10.254.239.20;%0a#  option routers rtr-239-0-1.example.org, rtr-239-0-2.example.org;%0a#}%0a%0a# This declaration allows BOOTP clients to get dynamic addresses,%0a# which we don't really recommend.%0a%0a#subnet 10.254.239.32 netmask 255.255.255.224 {%0a#  range dynamic-bootp 10.254.239.40 10.254.239.60;%0a#  option broadcast-address 10.254.239.31;%0a#  option routers rtr-239-32-1.example.org;%0a#}%0a%0a# A slightly different configuration for an internal subnet.%0a#subnet 10.5.5.0 netmask 255.255.255.224 {%0a#  range 10.5.5.26 10.5.5.30;%0a#  option domain-name-servers ns1.internal.example.org;%0a#  option domain-name "internal.example.org";%0a#  option routers 10.5.5.1;%0a#  option broadcast-address 10.5.5.31;%0a#  default-lease-time 600;%0a#  max-lease-time 7200;%0a#}%0a%0a# Hosts which require special configuration options can be listed in%0a# host statements.   If no address is specified, the address will be%0a# allocated dynamically (if possible), but the host-specific information%0a# will still come from the host declaration.%0a%0a#host passacaglia {%0a#  hardware ethernet 0:0:c0:5d:bd:95;%0a#  filename "vmunix.passacaglia";%0a#  server-name "toccata.fugue.com";%0a#}%0a%0a# Fixed IP addresses can also be specified for hosts.   These addresses%0a# should not also be listed as being available for dynamic assignment.%0a# Hosts for which fixed IP addresses have been specified can boot using%0a# BOOTP or DHCP.   Hosts for which no fixed address is specified can only%0a# be booted with DHCP, unless there is an address range on the subnet%0a# to which a BOOTP client is connected which has the dynamic-bootp flag%0a# set.%0a#host fantasia {%0a#  hardware ethernet 08:00:07:26:c0:a5;%0a#  fixed-address fantasia.fugue.com;%0a#}%0a%0a# You can declare a class of clients and then do address allocation%0a# based on that.   The example below shows a case where all clients%0a# in a certain class get addresses on the 10.17.224/24 subnet, and all%0a# other clients get addresses on the 10.0.29/24 subnet.%0a%0a#class "foo" {%0a#  match if substring (option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 4) = "SUNW";%0a#}%0a%0a#shared-network 224-29 {%0a#  subnet 10.17.224.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a#    option routers rtr-224.example.org;%0a#  }%0a#  subnet 10.0.29.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a#    option routers rtr-29.example.org;%0a#  }%0a#  pool {%0a#    allow members of "foo";%0a#    range 10.17.224.10 10.17.224.250;%0a#  }%0a#  pool {%0a#    deny members of "foo";%0a#    range 10.0.29.10 10.0.29.230;%0a#  }%0a#}@]%0a%0acat /etc/ltsp/dhcpd.conf%0a[@authoritative;%0a%0asubnet 192.168.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {%0a  range 192.168.0.20 192.168.0.250;%0a  option domain-name "ltspteste";%0a  option domain-name-servers 192.168.0.1;%0a  option broadcast-address 192.168.0.255;%0a  option routers 192.168.0.1;%0a  option subnet-mask 255.255.255.0;%0a  if substring( option vendor-class-identifier, 0, 9 ) = "PXEClient" {%0a    filename "/ltsp/i386/pxelinux.0";%0a  }%0a  else{%0a    filename "/ltsp/i386/nbi.img";%0a  }%0a  option root-path "/opt/ltsp/i386";%0a}@]%0a%0a%0a%0acat /etc/inetd.conf%0a[@# /etc/inetd.conf:  see inetd(8) for further informations.%0a#%0a# Internet superserver configuration database%0a#%0a#%0a# Lines starting with "#:LABEL:" or "#%3coff>#" should not%0a# be changed unless you know what you are doing!%0a#%0a# If you want to disable an entry so it isn't touched during%0a# package updates just comment it out with a single '#' character.%0a#%0a# Packages should modify this file by using update-inetd(8)%0a#%0a# %3cservice_name> %3csock_type> %3cproto> %3cflags> %3cuser> %3cserver_path> %3cargs>%0a#%0a#:INTERNAL: Internal services%0a#discard                stream  tcp     nowait  root    internal%0a#discard                dgram   udp     wait    root    internal%0a#daytime                stream  tcp     nowait  root    internal%0a#time           stream  tcp     nowait  root    internal%0a%0a#:STANDARD: These are standard services.%0a%0a#:BSD: Shell, login, exec and talk are BSD protocols.%0a%0a#:MAIL: Mail, news and uucp services.%0a%0a#:INFO: Info services%0a%0a#:BOOT: TFTP service is provided primarily for booting.  Most sites%0a#       run this only on machines acting as "boot servers."%0atftp           dgram   udp     wait    root  /usr/sbin/in.tftpd /usr/sbin/in.tftpd -s /var/lib/tftpboot%0a%0a#:RPC: RPC based services%0a%0a#:HAM-RADIO: amateur-radio services%0a%0a#:OTHER: Other services%0a%0a9571           stream  tcp     nowait  nobody /usr/sbin/tcpd /usr/sbin/ldminfod%0a9572                    stream  tcp     nowait  nobody /usr/sbin/tcpd /usr/sbin/nbdswapd@]%0a%0acat /etc/exports%0a[@# /etc/exports: the access control list for filesystems which may be exported%0a#               to NFS clients.  See exports(5).%0a#%0a# Example for NFSv2 and NFSv3:%0a# /srv/homes       hostname1(rw,sync) hostname2(ro,sync)%0a#%0a# Example for NFSv4:%0a# /srv/nfs4        gss/krb5i(rw,sync,fsid=0,crossmnt)%0a# /srv/nfs4/homes  gss/krb5i(rw,sync)%0a#%0a# Automatically added by ltsp-server%0a/opt/ltsp       *(ro,no_root_squash,async)@]%0a%0a%0aNo - /usr/share/doc/ltsp-server - tem uma pasta com alguns texto/docs interessantes de se ver.\\%0ahttp://idsorocaba.batemacumba.net/anexo/ltsp-server.tar.gz\\%0aDentro tem: [[workstation]] - [[dhcpd-dnsmasq]] - [[swap]]%0a
